 {:seon.task/name "roots-page-shows-each-branchs-drift-from-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner via root, 2026-11-14: \"make merging boring so it happens more and agents are just always integrating with main.\" The plan's measure is each branch's drift from main, shown on root's page and derived from branch bases, with no stored counter.\n\nWhat exists [M]:\n- seon.db/branch-base: the db at Datahike's merge-base of a branch and main. agent.clj:564 and writer.clj:19/89/360 already call it.\n- The writer answers {:op :branch-db} to the render proc, as the turns tile's ask-turns uses it (seon.render.proc).\n- A render cannot reach other branches. The turns tile works around that the same way, so both read branches through one mechanism. This is the core's \"render reads branches\", with the tile drawing my.root/turns-of's rows: one way, not two.\n\nDrift of branch b:\n- commits: (:max-tx main) - (:max-tx base), the transactions main took since b last synced;\n- age: the txInstant of main's head minus that of base's last transaction. Both are data, and no wall clock is read (docs/clocks.md).\n- Optional later: the datom count (seon.ctx/moved, O(changed)).\n\nRows: one per live branch, from the store's branch list (d/branches), each with its agent or session.\n\nEstimate (lower bound, as arithmetic):\n- On a main move, every branch's drift grows: O(B) row updates, B the live branches (~10-20).\n- A branch's merge-base is k commit-graph steps since it diverged, each a cached commit read: O(B*k) on a main move, with k reset to 0 on each sync.\n- On a commit to branch b, only b's row moves: O(k_b).\n- At B=20 and k<=50: under 1,000 commit reads per main move.\n- A test at 3 branches holds this as :growth :linear in branches, never in history.\n- Simpler: keep each branch's base as the point its last merge-main! or seon_done wrote (:seon.tx/merged), giving O(1) per branch. Measure both and pick by the 90/10 rule.\n\nProof: seon.render-test/roots-page-shows-each-branchs-drift-from-main. Main moves twice, and a branch that never synced reads 2 commits behind; it merges main and reads 0; a sibling branch stays at 2.\n\nOrder: after web's item 4 (deletions onto the core) and W-5. Needs a render that reads branches through the core.\n\nSimpler, found 17:30 [M, writer.clj:353-361]: the writer's :branch-db answer already carries :base (the merge-base db) and :base-t (main's t there). So drift needs no merge-base of its own: commits behind = (:max-tx main) - :base-t, and age = the txInstant gap between them. The branch Store that seon.render.proc/a-branch-head-is-a-store-a-render-derefs keeps holds both. This drops the O(B*k) design above, and the O(1) per-branch alternative too."
  :seon.task/kind :whole
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/roots-page-shows-each-branchs-drift-from-main}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render.proc/a-branch-head-is-a-store-a-render-derefs"}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-persons-chat-is-kind-plain-and-portrait-first"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-15 (docs/decisions.md, The page is a friendly chat): the person's view is for a non-technical 75-year-old, portrait-first (he reads it in the in-app browser pane, ~610 px): messages by default, what happened under the hood folded and one tap away, kind plain words, beautiful type; @ctx and debug stay technical and whole. Extends seon.render.ui/stage (stage-front, stage-now, stage-folds), seon.render.ui/steps and page-chrome; every change is page-only (CSS, :wire/for \"page\" parts, the frame), so the model's request stays byte-identical (seon.render-test/the-model-and-the-page-read-the-same-parts; context reviews).\n\nMeasured 2026-11-15 [M browser]: root's home 5.2-6.9 MB; NO render CSS on the page (0 <style>; the-css task below), so the stage's own rules never apply; the home carries the system's view (root-head counts, process, problems 3,458 eval errors, turns, root evals) and an agents list printing raw contract errors; the conversation opens with the system prompt, shows seon.turn/work! forms twice, a startup load error as a message from Seon, and 'start with --profile deepseek'; at 610 px the agents strip overflows and the bar wraps.\n\nOrder: (1) the CSS reaches the page (needs); (2) / is the person's conversation only: the system view moves to /root/debug, the checkout line to debug; (3) machinery folded: the system message, Seon's own evals, start-up errors and error maps under one 'Details' with the reason in one kind sentence; (4) portrait CSS: one column under 64rem, the bar one line, sessions behind a menu; (5) type: system-ui sans 18-20 px, line-height 1.6. Open for root: / follows the agent a tab last opened (docs/users.md:124, focus-tx); for a person, should / stay their conversation? Full plan and measures: the web session's notes (web-person-view-plan-2026-11-15).\n\nCOST: page-only; the home falls from ~6 MB to O(messages) (~1-2 KB each; lower bound the messages' text). Each step checked in the browser pane at its width.\n\nCites: `seon.render.ui/stage-front`, `seon.render.ui/steps`, `seon.render.ui/page-chrome`, `docs/render.md#the-page`, `docs/decisions.md#the-page-is-a-friendly-chat-what-the-model-was-sent-is-shown-whole`.\n\nThe owner's rulings since (docs/decisions.md, The page is a friendly chat): / is root's chat, always; the person never learns other agents exist, root manages them as their product and project manager and answers in one conversation; no sessions list and no system status in the person's view (both on debug, with the agents' pages and the checkout line); one fold per answer opens its steps; while work runs the waiting line shows the newest progress update live, every update one tap away (the update path from the working agents is flow's and root's, web draws it); theme follows the device; a failure is one kind sentence, its reason under Details; body type 19-20px; a message box that grows with its text, a clear Send button, Enter sends; the page opens on a fresh greeting with a link back to the previous conversation (a scroll back over the store, never the model's window); one user for now (the signed-in user). The build waits until web has talked the details through with the owner."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.render.ui{stage-front {:n 100, :order :linear, :ticks 2000000, :bytes 4000000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render.proc/a-page-keeps-its-renders-css-on-every-pass"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/the-persons-chat-holds-no-machinery-and-the-model-reads-it-whole}}
